---

<section id="motivation" class="py-16 sm:py-20 px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 max-w-7xl mx-auto">
  <div class="max-w-3xl mb-10">
    <h2 class="text-3xl sm:text-5xl font-bold tracking-tight uppercase leading-none">
      Faster, inspectable,{' '}
      <span class="font-serif italic font-normal normal-case text-[#FF4F18]">and spliceable.</span>
    </h2>
    <p class="mt-4 text-lg sm:text-xl text-ink/80 font-medium">
      I started this project to see how much faster Protobuf's wire format could be on modern CPUs,
      and ended up making it work directly on Go structs and TypeScript objects too.
    </p>
  </div>

  <div class="border-t-2 border-ink divide-y-2 divide-ink/20">
    <!-- 1. FASTER -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          No byte-by-byte varint loops. No two-pass encoding
        </h3>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          When Protobuf reads a <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/LEB128" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link">LEB128</a> varint, it has to check the top bit of every single byte in a loop until it finds the end of the number. And when it writes a nested message, it has to walk the whole struct tree twice: once to add up the byte sizes, and a second time to write the bytes out. OmniBuf changes the integer encoding so the first byte tells the CPU how many bytes to read (using a single <a href="https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Find_first_set" target="_blank" rel="noopener noreferrer" class="doc-link"><code>TZCNT</code></a> instruction), and encoders reserve a 2-byte length slot for nested messages so they only have to walk the struct once.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="text-acid font-bold">5.9x faster encode vs Go Protobuf</div>
          <div class="text-white font-bold">Up to 7.3x faster decode (2.3x fresh alloc)</div>
          <div class="text-paper/75 text-sm">Single-pass forward encoding</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 2. INSPECTABLE WITHOUT A SCHEMA -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Inspect packets without a schema &amp; use normal structs
        </h3>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Protobuf only has a handful of wire types, so strings, byte slices, nested messages, and packed arrays all get serialized as <code>Wire Type 2 (LEN)</code>. If you inspect a raw Protobuf packet without the <code>.proto</code> file, a tool like <code>protoscope</code> has to guess what each field actually is. OmniBuf uses a 4-bit wire type (16 types instead of 6) plus 1-byte collection headers, which adds about 7% to our 11-field test packet so a decoder can recover the exact wire type and value of every present field (though enum label names, omitted defaults, and inactive oneof branches still require a schema).
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="font-bold text-ink">Schema-less value decoding</div>
          <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
            + Optional Field #0 field names for named JSON output
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 3. READ OR SPLICE WITHOUT DECODING -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Read, drop, or replace individual fields on raw bytes
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          Since every integer and container starts with its byte length, a decoder can skip over
          fields it doesn't care about by adding the byte length and jumping to the next tag. If you
          use the generated <code>Opaque</code> wrapper in a proxy or router, you can read one field
          out of a packet or update a field in place without unpacking the rest of the message.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-[#FAF8F2] border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="font-bold text-ink">Header-hop field skipping</div>
          <div class="text-ink/80 text-sm">
            <code>LocateField</code> &bull; <code>SpliceFields</code> &bull; 0 allocs
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 4. REPLACE STRUCT-DERIVED JSON WITHOUT .PROTO -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Replace struct-derived JSON without a .proto build step
        </h3>
        <!-- prettier-ignore -->
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          A lot of services use JSON simply because defining a <a href="/go#struct-tags" class="doc-link">Go struct</a> or a <a href="/es#define-message" class="doc-link">TypeScript type</a> is easier than setting up <code>.proto</code> files and a code generator. OmniBuf lets you do the same thing with <code>omnibuf:"1"</code> struct tags in Go or <code>defineMessage()</code> in TypeScript. The encoded bytes are less than half the size of JSON and 5x to 11x faster to encode, 64-bit integers and byte slices don't need special string encoding, and you can enable <strong>Field #0</strong> if you still want field names included in the packet.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-ink text-paper border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base space-y-1.5">
          <div class="text-acid font-bold">No .proto or codegen required</div>
          <div class="text-white font-bold">5x–11x faster than struct JSON</div>
          <div class="text-paper/75 text-sm">
            Go <code>omnibuf:"1"</code> &bull; TS <code>defineMessage</code>
          </div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <!-- 5. ALL THE BENEFITS OF PROTOBUF -->
    <div class="py-8 sm:py-10 grid lg:grid-cols-12 gap-6 lg:gap-8 items-center">
      <div class="lg:col-span-8 space-y-3">
        <h3 class="text-2xl sm:text-3xl font-bold uppercase tracking-tight leading-snug">
          Keep your .proto files, schema evolution, and compact payloads
        </h3>
        <p class="text-base sm:text-lg text-ink/90 leading-relaxed max-w-[60ch]">
          If you already have a Protobuf workflow, you can keep using your existing{' '}
          <code>.proto</code> files and generated structs. Field numbers, adding and deprecating
          fields over time, and merging two messages by concatenating their bytes (
          <code>append(msgA, msgB...)</code>) all work the same way as Protobuf.
        </p>
      </div>
      <div class="lg:col-span-4">
        <div class="p-5 bg-acid text-ink border-2 border-ink rounded-md font-mono text-sm sm:text-base font-bold space-y-1">
          <div>✓ Existing .proto schemas</div>
          <div>✓ Safe field evolution</div>
          <div>✓ append(msgA, msgB...) merge</div>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>
  </div>
</section>
